<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        ValidationRule.Description is capped at 255 characters. The full rationale below is
        operationally important, so it was moved here rather than trimmed; a comment is
        repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API. Do not paste this back into
        <description>.

        REWRITTEN 2026-08-28, replacing the version that gated on Opportunity.UW_Approved__c.
        User request: principal approval moved off the Opportunity and onto the deal's
        Underwriting__c child record (approvalProcesses/Underwriting__c.Underwriting_Approval).
        This rule now gates on the CHILD RECORD'S OWN Stage__c instead.

        🔴 THE PREVIOUS VERSION'S "SAFE BY CONSTRUCTION" CLAIM IS RETRACTED, IN PLACE, NOT
        DELETED. It used to read:
            "Safe against the legitimate route by construction: Underwriting_Approval's
            finalApprovalActions sets StageName = 'LOI' and UW_Approved__c = true in the same
            action set / same record save, so the flag is already true when this rule
            evaluates..."
        That argument is now FALSE. The new Underwriting__c approval cannot write StageName on
        the Opportunity at all (Opportunity__c is a Lookup on Underwriting__c, not a
        Master-Detail - an approval process can only field-update its own record or a
        Master-Detail parent). Initiate LOI is now a plain StageAdvanceService.advance() stage
        write, gated ONLY by this rule reading the child's Stage__c across the lookup.
        The previous version's "Known, accepted: UW_Approved__c is never cleared" paragraph is
        ALSO retracted - that field is no longer what this rule reads.

        🔴 WHY THE FORMULA NAMES BOTH 'Approved' AND 'Completed' - THIS IS THE LOAD-BEARING
        PART, NOT COSMETIC. LOI_Approval's finalRejectionActions fires Set_Stage_Underwriting
        (workflows/Opportunity.workflow-meta.xml), returning a rejected deal from LOI back to
        StageName = 'Underwriting'. By the time that happens, the deal's Underwriting record has
        USUALLY ALREADY been walked forward to its terminal Stage__c = 'Completed'
        (Underwriting__c.Move_to_Completed, gated at Stage__c = 'Approved',
        flexipages/Underwriting_Record_Page.flexipage-meta.xml). If this formula tested ONLY
        <> 'Approved', the deal would be PERMANENTLY STRANDED the second time the driver clicks
        Initiate LOI: 'Completed' is the terminal of RecordStageAdvanceService.
        UNDERWRITING_NEXT_STAGE, nothing targets 'Approved' from there, and Stage__c is
        <restricted>true</restricted> so a manual DML repoint is the only way out. Naming both
        terminal-side values closes that hole. Same list-both-then-accept shape
        Completed_LOI_Before_PSA already uses in this folder (see that file's own header for the
        precedent in more detail) - here there is no "then-drop" phase, both values are
        permanent terminals of the SAME object's SAME field, not a migration in flight.

        🔴 NO NOT(ISBLANK(Primary_Underwriting__c)) GUARD - DELIBERATE, UNLIKE
        Completed_LOI_Before_PSA'S BLANK-LOOKUP EXEMPTION. That sibling rule exempts a blank
        lookup because a DIFFERENT sibling rule (Approved_LOI_Before_PSA) already owns the
        blank-lookup case for that transition, and two error messages for one problem is worse
        than one. NO SUCH SIBLING RULE EXISTS HERE for the Underwriting -> LOI transition. A
        deal with no linked Underwriting record (Primary_Underwriting__c blank) must therefore
        stay FAIL-CLOSED: TEXT(Primary_Underwriting__r.Stage__c) evaluates to '' when the lookup
        is blank, and '' is neither 'Approved' nor 'Completed', so this rule correctly BLOCKS
        that case on its own. Do NOT add a blank-lookup exemption here by copying the sibling
        pattern - there is nothing else in this rule pack that would catch a blank lookup if
        this rule stopped doing it.

        WHY TEXT(...) NOT ISPICKVAL(...). Repo standing rule for every cross-object stage
        compare of this shape (see Completed_LOI_Before_PSA / RecordStageAdvanceService class
        header): ISPICKVAL resolves its literal against the picklist's value set at DEPLOY TIME,
        which is fragile for a cross-object reference and inconsistent elsewhere in this org's
        picklist metadata. TEXT() is a plain string compare and carries no such risk.

        WHICH WRITER THIS RULE ACTUALLY MEETS. StageName = 'LOI' from 'Underwriting' is reached
        by StageAdvanceService.advance() via NEXT_STAGE's 'Underwriting' => 'LOI' mapping
        (Opportunity.Initiate_LOI quick action), now a PLAIN stage write with no approval
        submission branch - the old "if (o.StageName == 'Underwriting') { submit for approval }"
        branch was deleted in the same change that deactivated Opportunity.Underwriting_Approval.
        advance()'s setStage() catches DmlException and rethrows getDmlMessage(0), so this rule's
        errorMessage surfaces verbatim in the user's toast with no Apex change required.

        GRANDFATHERING. This rule is ISCHANGED(StageName)-scoped like its siblings: it fires
        only on the transition into LOI, never on a deal already sitting there.
    -->
    <fullName>Underwriting_Approved_Before_LOI</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Blocks stage entry to LOI unless the primary Underwriting record has reached Approved or Completed. Fails closed if Primary_Underwriting__c is blank. See the XML comment in this file for full rationale.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    ISCHANGED(StageName),
    ISPICKVAL(StageName, &apos;LOI&apos;),
    TEXT(Primary_Underwriting__r.Stage__c) &lt;&gt; &apos;Approved&apos;,
    TEXT(Primary_Underwriting__r.Stage__c) &lt;&gt; &apos;Completed&apos;
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorDisplayField>StageName</errorDisplayField>
    <errorMessage>This deal cannot enter LOI until its Underwriting record has been approved by the principals. Open the deal&apos;s Underwriting record and use Submit for Approval.</errorMessage>
</ValidationRule>
